    title: 'Historical Timeline of Afghan Mining',
    subtitle: 'Chronology of Mineral Extraction and Trade from Antiquity to the Present',
    
    overview: `Afghanistan is one of the oldest continuously mined regions on Earth. This timeline sets out the chronology of mineral extraction and trade from the lapis lazuli mines of Badakhshan that supplied ancient Egypt to the modern discovery of world-class copper and lithium deposits, and how that story has shaped civilizations, empires, and global trade.

The earliest evidence of mining dates to 4000 BCE in Badakhshan Province, where lapis lazuli extraction began and continues to this day—making these the oldest continuously operated mines in human history. Archaeological evidence confirms that Afghan lapis supplied civilizations across the ancient world, from Mesopotamia to the Indus Valley, creating trade networks spanning 3,000 kilometers. The mines provided lapis for Tutankhamun's death mask (1323 BCE), Cleopatra's cosmetics, and Roman jewelry, establishing Afghanistan's reputation as a source of the world's finest gemstones.

Medieval periods saw continued gemstone mining, with emerald extraction in Panjshir dating to at least 1000 CE. The Silk Road trade routes carried Afghan minerals to markets across Asia, Europe, and Africa, establishing Afghanistan as a key node in global trade networks. Mining supported local economies while providing materials for luxury goods, religious artifacts, and royal collections throughout the Islamic world and beyond.

Modern geological exploration began in the 19th century with British and Russian surveys identifying mineral potential. Soviet geological campaigns in the 1960s-1980s conducted comprehensive mapping and identified major deposits including Mes Aynak copper and Hajigak iron. These surveys created the foundation for modern understanding of Afghanistan's mineral wealth, though political instability delayed development for decades.

The 21st century has brought renewed focus on mineral development, with comprehensive surveys by the USGS and other international organizations quantifying Afghanistan's $30+ trillion mineral endowment. Modern exploration techniques have revealed world-class deposits including potentially the world's largest lithium reserves, positioning Afghanistan as a critical player in global mineral supply chains for the energy transition and technology sectors.`,

