    sections: [
      {
        title: 'Comprehensive Geological Mapping & Field Exploration',
        content: `Soviet geological campaigns employed systematic exploration methodologies combining traditional field mapping with emerging geophysical techniques. Teams of 200+ geologists, geophysicists, and support staff conducted comprehensive surveys across Afghanistan's 34 provinces, creating detailed geological maps at 1:50,000 and 1:200,000 scales. Field mapping identified surface exposures, structural features, and mineralization indicators, while geophysical surveys including magnetic, gravity, and electrical methods revealed subsurface geological structures. The comprehensive approach created the most detailed geological database of Afghanistan's mineral potential before modern satellite imagery and advanced geophysical techniques. Geological reports documented over 1,400 mineral occurrences, with detailed descriptions of deposit types, grades, and tonnages. The systematic methodology established standards for mineral exploration that continue to guide modern development efforts.`,
        icon: MapPin,
        alignment: 'left'
      },
      {
        title: 'Major Deposit Discoveries & Resource Quantification',
        content: `Soviet surveys identified world-class deposits across multiple mineral categories, fundamentally altering understanding of Afghanistan's mineral potential. Mes Aynak copper deposit discovery revealed 125m tons of copper ore with grades averaging 2.3%—significantly higher than global averages. Hajigak iron deposit identification revealed 2.2 billion tons of high-grade magnetite ore suitable for direct reduction steelmaking. Additional discoveries included extensive coal reserves totaling 4 billion tons across 35 basins, chromite deposits with 30 million tons proven reserves, and numerous gemstone occurrences. The surveys documented Afghanistan's exceptional mineral diversity, identifying deposits across all major mineral categories including base metals, precious metals, industrial minerals, and energy resources. Resource estimates created during this period provided foundation for modern development planning, with many deposits remaining undeveloped awaiting favorable conditions for systematic exploitation.`,
        icon: Mountain,
        alignment: 'right'
      },
      {
        title: 'Technical Infrastructure & Capacity Building',
        content: `Soviet geological programs established technical infrastructure supporting mineral exploration and development. Geological laboratories equipped with analytical equipment enabled comprehensive geochemical analysis of samples, while core storage facilities preserved drilling samples for future reference. Training programs developed Afghan geologists and technicians, building technical capacity that continues to benefit Afghanistan's mineral sector. The programs created geological databases, maps, and reports that remain valuable resources for modern exploration efforts. Technical infrastructure including drilling equipment, geophysical instruments, and laboratory facilities supported comprehensive exploration activities. The capacity building efforts created foundation for Afghanistan's geological expertise, with many trained professionals continuing to contribute to mineral development decades later.`,
        icon: Building2,
        alignment: 'left'
      },
      {
        title: 'Legacy & Modern Application',
        content: `The Soviet geological legacy provides essential foundation for modern mineral development, with geological maps, reports, and databases informing contemporary exploration strategies. Modern exploration builds upon Soviet discoveries, utilizing advanced techniques including satellite imagery, airborne geophysics, and deep drilling to expand understanding of known deposits and identify new targets. The comprehensive geological knowledge created during this period enables informed investment decisions, guiding development of Afghanistan's $30+ trillion mineral endowment. Historical data integration with modern exploration techniques creates powerful tools for mineral discovery and development planning. The Soviet geological legacy demonstrates value of systematic exploration, providing model for comprehensive mineral resource assessment that continues to guide Afghanistan's mineral development strategy.`,
        icon: FileText,
        alignment: 'right'
      }
    ],
