    sections: [
      {
        title: 'Badakhshan Lapis Lazuli: Millennia of Excellence',
        content: `The Badakhshan lapis lazuli mines represent humanity's oldest continuously operated mines, with archaeological evidence confirming extraction beginning around 4000 BCE. These mines supplied the world's finest lapis lazuli for over millennia, creating trade networks spanning 3,000+ kilometers connecting Badakhshan to ancient Egypt, Mesopotamia, and the Indus Valley. The distinctive deep blue color with golden pyrite inclusions created "Persian blue" grade commanding premium prices throughout history. Lapis from Badakhshan adorned Tutankhamun's death mask (1323 BCE), Cleopatra's cosmetics, Roman jewelry, and Renaissance masterpieces. Powdered lapis created ultramarine pigment worth more than gold during the Renaissance. The mines continue operating today, maintaining their status as the world's primary source of fine lapis lazuli.`,
        icon: Gem,
        alignment: 'left'
      },
      {
        title: 'Panjshir Emeralds: Discovery, Quality Characteristics & Market Position',
        content: `Panjshir emeralds were discovered at the Buzmal mine in early 1970s, located east of Dest-e-Rewat village, marking beginning of commercial emerald production. Soviet geologists found the deposit in 1970, with systematic surveys beginning in mid-1970s (Rossovskiy et al., 1976; Abdullah et al., 1977). Panjshir emeralds exhibit exceptional color characteristics with chromium content creating vivid green hues (hue: 7.5G to 10G) with saturation levels comparable to Colombian emeralds. The emeralds display high clarity with fewer inclusions than many sources, with crystal sizes reaching 10+ carats in exceptional specimens. Gemological analysis reveals chromium-to-vanadium ratios creating distinctive color profiles, with refractive indices of 1.577-1.583 and specific gravity of 2.67-2.78. The finest Panjshir emeralds command premium prices in international markets, with quality grades matching or exceeding Colombian standards. Source: GIA, "Emeralds of the Panjshir Valley, Afghanistan" (1991); Soviet geological reports (1976-1982).`,
        icon: Gem,
        alignment: 'right'
      },
      {
        title: 'Jegdalek Rubies: Historical Production & Gemological Characteristics',
        content: `Jegdalek ruby mines have been worked for more than 700 years, with historical records documenting ruby sales to Kublai Khan during 1200s. The mines produce rubies hosted in corundum-bearing marble formations, with most material consisting of semitransparent pink sapphire suitable for cabochon cutting. Facet-grade ruby represents smaller percentage but commands premium prices. Gemological analysis reveals chromium content creating red hues, with color saturation varying significantly across deposits. The rubies display characteristic inclusions including rutile needles and zircon crystals, with refractive indices of 1.762-1.770 and specific gravity of 3.97-4.05. Historical Arabic writings document Jegdalek rubies dating to 10th-14th centuries, with travelers including Istakhri (951 CE), Ibn Haukal (978 CE), and al-Biruni (d. 1050 CE) describing the deposits. Source: GIA, "Ruby and Sapphire from Jegdalek, Afghanistan" (2000); Historical Arabic geographical works.`,
        icon: Gem,
        alignment: 'left'
      },
      {
        title: 'Gemstone Cutting & Polishing: Traditional Techniques & Modern Methods',
        content: `Afghan gemstone cutting and polishing techniques have evolved over millennia, with traditional methods refined through generations of craftsmen. Lapis lazuli cutting utilizes specialized techniques to maximize color display while preserving pyrite inclusions that enhance value. Emerald cutting requires careful orientation to maximize color saturation and minimize inclusion visibility, with traditional methods emphasizing preservation of crystal size. Ruby cutting focuses on color enhancement through precise faceting angles, with traditional cabochon cutting preserving material with inclusions. Modern cutting combines traditional knowledge with advanced techniques including precision faceting machines and computer-aided design. The cutting industry supports thousands of artisans, with skills passed through generations creating distinctive Afghan cutting styles recognized globally. Quality grading systems have evolved to recognize Afghan gemstone characteristics, with international laboratories establishing standards for Afghan materials.`,
        icon: Settings,
        alignment: 'right'
      }
    ],
