        content: `Ultramarine pigment commanded exceptional prices in Renaissance Europe, with documented prices showing it costing up to 100 times more than other blue pigments including azurite and smalt. The high cost limited ultramarine use to the most prestigious commissions, with artists and patrons carefully considering when to use the expensive material versus cheaper alternatives. Renaissance account books document ultramarine purchases, with prices recorded in detail for accounting purposes. The economic value of ultramarine made it a significant investment for patrons, with its use in paintings demonstrating both artistic commitment and financial resources. The market dynamics demonstrate how luxury materials commanded premium prices in Renaissance Europe, with Afghan lapis lazuli maintaining its value throughout the trade network from mining region to artistic center.`,
