    sections: [
      {
        title: 'Tillya Tepe Bactrian Gold: Viktor Sarianidi Excavation (1978)',
        content: `Tillya Tepe (Golden Hill) is a significant archaeological site in Jowzjan Province, northern Afghanistan, excavated in 1978 by a Soviet-Afghan team led by archaeologist Viktor Sarianidi. The site contains the Bactrian Gold hoard, a collection of approximately 20,600 ornaments, coins, and artifacts made of gold, silver, and ivory, dated to around the 1st century BCE-1st century CE. The hoard was discovered in six burial mounds containing rich jewelry, necklaces set with semi-precious stones, belts, medallions, and crowns. The gold artifacts demonstrate sophisticated craftsmanship and goldworking techniques, with intricate designs incorporating gemstones and decorative elements. The discovery provides direct archaeological evidence of gold extraction, processing, and use in ancient Afghanistan, demonstrating the material's importance in ancient societies. The Bactrian Gold hoard represents one of the most significant archaeological gold discoveries in Central Asia, providing insight into ancient gold mining and trade. Source: Viktor Sarianidi excavation (1978); Archaeological evidence; Bactrian Gold documentation.`,
        icon: Archive,
        alignment: 'left'
      },
      {
        title: 'Badakhshan Gold Veins: Soviet-Afghan Surveys (1960s)',
        content: `Detailed geological reconnaissance was conducted during the 1960s by joint Soviet and Afghan programs in Badakhshan Province, focusing on gold-bearing quartz vein systems. The largest and most studied gold-bearing quartz vein system in the region is located 360km north of Kabul in Badakhshan Province. Prospects like Veka Dur were investigated with trench and adit sampling, documenting gold occurrence in quartz veins. The geological surveys identified gold mineralization associated with quartz veins in metamorphic rocks, with systematic exploration documenting deposit characteristics. The Badakhshan gold veins represent primary gold deposits distinct from placer deposits, requiring different extraction methods. The 1960s surveys provided foundation for understanding Afghanistan's gold potential, documenting both primary vein deposits and placer occurrences. Source: Soviet-Afghan geological programs (1960s); Badakhshan gold documentation.`,
        icon: Mountain,
        alignment: 'right'
      },
      {
        title: 'North Takhar Placer Deposits: Samti Reserve Estimates',
        content: `The North Takhar Area contains one of Afghanistan's two largest gold placers, with the Samti reserve estimated at over 30,000 kilograms in Soviet geological assessments. Modern reassessment using satellite technology conducted between 2009-2011 by USGS estimated 20,927 kg at Samti, providing updated resource estimates. The North Takhar Area is located in Takhar Province, northeastern Afghanistan, with placer gold deposits formed through erosion and concentration of gold from primary sources. The placer deposits represent alluvial gold concentrations in river systems, with gold particles concentrated through natural processes. The modern reassessment utilized advanced satellite imagery and remote sensing techniques to refine resource estimates, demonstrating evolution of exploration methods. Source: USGS Open-File Report 2013-1180; Soviet geological assessments; Satellite technology reassessment (2009-2011).`,
        icon: Target,
        alignment: 'left'
      },
      {
        title: 'Two Principal Placer Gold Regions: Distribution & Characteristics',
        content: `Afghanistan contains two principal placer gold regions according to USGS assessments. The northeastern region encompasses western Badakhshan and northern Takhar Provinces, with significant placer deposits including Samti reserve. The southwestern region includes Badakhshan, Ghazni, Kandahar, and Zabul Provinces, with placer gold occurrences documented through geological surveys. The identification of two principal placer regions provides framework for understanding gold distribution and exploration targeting. Placer deposits represent secondary gold concentrations formed through erosion and transport of primary gold sources, with gold particles concentrated in alluvial systems. The regional distribution demonstrates gold occurrence across multiple provinces, indicating widespread gold potential. Source: USGS assessments; Geological survey documentation.`,
        icon: MapPin,
        alignment: 'right'
      }],
    
