        content: `An even older 5,000-year-old Bronze Age site beneath the Buddhist level at Mes Aynak includes an ancient copper smelter, demonstrating industrial-scale copper exploitation during antiquity. The smelter provides direct archaeological evidence of copper extraction and processing techniques used 5,000 years ago. Colossal deposits of slag indicate extensive ancient smelting operations, with waste material accumulation demonstrating long-term copper production. The Bronze Age smelter predates the Buddhist settlement by over 2,000 years, demonstrating continuous or repeated copper extraction at the site across millennia. The archaeological evidence confirms Mes Aynak as one of the world's oldest copper mining sites, with extraction beginning during the Bronze Age period. Source: Archaeological excavations; National Geographic; Mes Aynak documentation.`,
