        content: `The Buddhist settlement at Mes Aynak peaked between the 5th and 7th centuries AD, with the Buddhist complex dating from the third to eighth centuries AD. Coins from Alchon Hun rulers found at the site confirm their presence around 450-500 CE, providing specific dating evidence. The Buddhist complex represents one of the most important archaeological sites along the Silk Road, with strategic importance connected to copper resources. A period of slow decline began in the 8th century, with final abandonment occurring 200 years later around the 10th century. The Buddhist settlement demonstrates the site's continued importance during medieval periods, with copper resources supporting settlement development. Archaeological evidence shows the Buddhist community utilized and potentially managed copper extraction operations. Source: Archaeological dating; Coins from Alchon Hun rulers (450-500 CE); Buddhist settlement documentation.`,
