        content: `Second-century archaeological material from Palmyra provides evidence of Afghanistan's maritime trade connections during the 2nd century CE. Palmyra, located in modern Syria, served as important trading center connecting Mediterranean and Asian trade routes. Archaeological finds from Palmyra demonstrate Afghan commodities reaching Mediterranean markets through maritime and overland routes. The Palmyra evidence confirms continued maritime trade connections during the 2nd century, with Afghan products integrated into broader trade networks. The archaeological material provides physical evidence complementing written documentation, demonstrating material culture exchange. Source: Palmyra archaeological evidence (2nd century CE); Archaeological documentation.`,
