        title: 'Data Sources: Soviet, USGS, and National',
        content: `Historical data come from Soviet geological and production records, USGS and other international assessments, and national reporting (ministry, EITI, project disclosures). Soviet archives provide pre-1990 resource and production data for many commodities; USGS work from 2007 onward provides modern resource estimates and deposit-level figures. National reporting adds licensing, revenue, and where available production and employment. Gaps exist for some periods and commodities; for example, artisanal production is often estimated, and some Soviet series are incomplete for certain minerals. Source attribution is provided in timeline entries where relevant so that users can distinguish between robust and approximate data. When multiple sources exist for the same figure (e.g. Mes Aynak copper), the timeline uses the most widely cited official estimate and notes the source.`,
